  "prompt": "Simplify $1 - \\sin^2\\theta$. It equals $\\cos^n\\theta$. What is $n$?",
  "answerType": "numeric",
  "primaryAnswer": "2",
  "alternativeAnswers": [],
  "acceptanceMode": "normalized",
  "hint": "Start from the identity $\\sin^2\\theta + \\cos^2\\theta = 1$.",
  "hints": [
    {
      "level": "nudge",
      "text": "Start from the identity $\\sin^2\\theta + \\cos^2\\theta = 1$."
    },
    {
      "level": "guide",
      "text": "Subtract $\\sin^2\\theta$ from both sides: $\\cos^2\\theta = 1 - \\sin^2\\theta$."
    },
    {
      "level": "reveal",
      "text": "So $1 - \\sin^2\\theta = \\cos^2\\theta$ and $n = 2$."
    }
  ],
  "explanation": [
    "The Pythagorean identity says $\\sin^2\\theta + \\cos^2\\theta = 1$.",
    "Move $\\sin^2\\theta$ to the other side: $\\cos^2\\theta = 1 - \\sin^2\\theta$.",
    "So the expression simplifies to $\\cos^2\\theta$, and the power is $n = 2$."
  ],
  "alternativeExplanations": [
    {
      "title": "Test it with real numbers",
      "steps": [
        "Take θ = 30°: sin 30° = 0.5, so 1 − 0.5² = 1 − 0.25 = 0.75.",
        "And cos 30° ≈ 0.866, so cos² 30° ≈ 0.75. They match, so the power is 2, not 1."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Think of a full pie",
      "steps": [
        "The identity says sin² and cos² are two slices that together make one whole pie.",
        "Take the sin² slice away from the whole pie and what is left is exactly the cos² slice."
      ]
    }
  ],
